        <!-- In-Depth Review -->
        <div class="bg-[#111] border border-white/5 rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Our Verdict</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">ServerPeacock occupies a useful but narrow niche in the EU mid-market VPS segment: independent Dutch operator, IPv6 default, direct-wallet crypto billing, four transatlantic datacenters, EUR pricing. The pitch will resonate strongly with EU-based indie developers, privacy-respecting small businesses, and buyers who specifically want a non-hyperscaler EU headquarters for GDPR / data-residency reasons. It will not resonate with buyers chasing the absolute cheapest $/GB (RackNerd / BuyVM beat it), buyers needing hyperscaler-grade managed services (AWS / GCP / Azure / Vultr are the right picks), or buyers in APAC (no HK / SG / Tokyo nodes).</p>
          <p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">The biggest differentiator vs the budget tier is <strong>billing surface area</strong>. Direct-wallet Monero on a Dutch-registered provider is a meaningful configuration for buyers who have already decided they don't want card-on-file with a hyperscaler and don't want a US-based reseller in the chain. Combine that with IPv6 on every plan (no upsell) and SEPA for EU customers, and ServerPeacock becomes the closest "EU budget EU-native" pick on this site. The provider is small enough that you should still keep off-site backups and verify the EU datacenters' actual location vs claimed location at signup — but the operational pattern is well-documented in the LowEndTalk / Reddit / NFO Forums threads over 2018-2026.</p>
